---

<PageShell
  title="Conformance - ULC specification"
  description="How ULC computes a record's conformance level: three grades (Core, Standard, Full) above an Incomplete floor, why the cut points mirror a Division 26 construction specification, and why a fixture is graded only on what its form requires, and how the eight reference records grade."
>
  <SpecDocsLayout>
    <h1 class="page-h1">Conformance.</h1>
    <p class="page-intro">
      A ULC record's conformance level is computed, never declared. The reference
      builder grades each record from the data it actually carries and stamps the
      result; the validator recomputes it and checks it, so there is no way to
      assert or inflate a level by hand. The level is one of two computed views:
      its sibling, Product Achievements, answers "what is the product documented
      for?" and has <a href="/docs/achievements" class="cf-link">its own page</a>.
    </p>

    {/* ── Three grades, and a floor ─────────────────────────────────────── */}
    <section class="cf-section">
      <h2 class="cf-h2">Three grades, and a floor</h2>
      <p>
        ULC has three conformance grades, Core, Standard, and Full. Each adds a
        deeper class of evidence than the one below, and a record earns the
        highest one its data fully supports.
      </p>
      <dl class="cf-levels">
        <dt>Core</dt>
        <dd>
          The entry grade: the minimum a fixture must carry to be specified and
          ordered at all. Mandatory product data (identity, classification, the
          headline photometric and electrical values, one-line colorimetry), an
          attached cutsheet, and a recognized safety listing. It sits below the selection-grade data and
          the accredited test evidence the higher grades add.
        </dd>
        <dt>Standard</dt>
        <dd>
          Core plus the full LM-79 selection-grade specification: maximum
          intensity, symmetry and coordinate system, materials, test conditions,
          an LM-79 attestation, a lumen-maintenance framework, and the conditional
          rows the fixture's form calls for. This is the data a Division 26
          lighting specification selects products on, which is why it ranks above
          Core. A Standard record is missing only the
          accredited third-party test evidence that lifts it to Full.
        </dd>
        <dt>Full</dt>
        <dd>
          Everything: Standard plus the independently-certified characterization
          an accredited test report supplies. Zonal lumens, an operating point,
          measurement uncertainty, applied corrections, instrumentation depth, a
          method-backed lumen-maintenance projection, and, for white-light
          fixtures, TM-30 color detail.
        </dd>
      </dl>
      <p>
        Below the grades sits the floor, <strong>Incomplete</strong>: where a
        record sits until it meets Core. Not a grade. A single missing Core
        requirement holds it here, even one that already carries Standard- or
        Full-grade data elsewhere. The tooling never refuses it: a record with
        nothing but its identity still grades Incomplete, and always carries a
        roadmap to Core. Identity is the one thing a record must carry to exist
        at all (manufacturer, catalog model, and family id); a record without it
        is malformed, not Incomplete.
      </p>
      <p>
        Grading is a cumulative gate: a record earns a grade only when it meets
        every applicable requirement of that grade and of the grades below it.
        Every requirement still outstanding, at every grade up to Full, becomes a
        roadmap entry naming the field, its source document, and the governing
        standard. Beside those hard gaps, the validator also suggests a
        non-gating enrichment roadmap: optional depth (power factor, flicker,
        alpha-opic metrics, UGR, and similar) that deepens a datasheet but never
        moves the grade.
      </p>
      <p>
        These cut points are not arbitrary. They recover how a Division 26
        construction specification (the CSI MasterFormat division that governs
        electrical and lighting work) escalates the evidence it demands of a
        manufacturer as the specification tightens. Two axes order the ladder.
        <strong>Evidence depth</strong>: each grade asks for a deeper class of
        characterization, which is why Full sits above the datasheet-grade
        Standard band. <strong>Conditional applicability</strong>: a
        specification asks for a metric only when the fixture's form calls for
        it, the rule the next section makes concrete.
      </p>
      <p class="cf-note">
        A conformance level is a data-completeness grade, never a safety
        certification: the Core safety-listing gate checks that a listing claim
        is present, not that a third party has verified it.
      </p>
    </section>

    {/* ── Graded only on what applies ─────────────────────────────────── */}
    <section class="cf-section">
      <h2 class="cf-h2">Graded only on what applies</h2>
      <p>
        A requirement only counts when it applies to the fixture's form. When it
        does not, it is dropped from grading entirely, never counted as missing.
        That is what keeps the grade fair: a fixture is measured only against what
        a specifier would actually ask of that kind of fixture.
      </p>
      <ul class="cf-list">
        <li>
          An <strong>indoor</strong> fixture is never asked for a BUG rating or an
          IP rating. Those are outdoor metrics; their absence never lowers its
          level.
        </li>
        <li>
          A <strong>color-mixing RGBW</strong> fixture is never asked for a single
          CRI figure. It is specified by its color gamut, not one number measured
          against a reference white.
        </li>
        <li>
          A <strong>DALI, DMX, or wireless</strong> fixture is never asked for an
          analog dimming range. Its dimming is commanded externally and is not
          printed on the cutsheet.
        </li>
        <li>
          An <strong>architectural uplight</strong> (in-ground or facade) is
          graded on its beam, not on an area distribution it does not have.
        </li>
        <li>
          An <strong>exit sign</strong> is graded against the sign profile:
          legend, illumination mode, battery, and UL 924 evidence, never the
          LM-79 photometry its spec sheet does not carry. A <strong>dedicated
          emergency luminaire</strong> keeps the normal profile minus luminaire
          efficacy, plus the emergency gates.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The class profiles take this rule to its conclusion. The product class is
        derived from the record's primary category: an exit sign grades against
        the sign dataset (the architectural photometric rows are dropped entirely,
        and Full is anchored on UL 924 test-report depth), and a dedicated
        emergency luminaire grades against the normal profile minus luminaire
        efficacy, plus the emergency gates. A UL 924 listing, read from the
        attestation ledger and never a boolean field, satisfies the core
        safety-listing requirement. A sign-only manufacturer reaches the grade its
        spec sheets actually support instead of being stranded at Incomplete for
        lacking an LM-79 report it never produces.
      </p>
      <p>
        So an honest datasheet is never demoted for a value its fixture has no
        reason to carry.
      </p>
    </section>

    {/* ── The eight reference records ─────────────────────────────────── */}
    <section class="cf-section">
      <h2 class="cf-h2">The eight reference records</h2>
      <p>
        The <a href="/downloads#examples" class="cf-link">eight canonical
        records</a> show grading on real cutsheets: five architectural records
        across the four authoring patterns, and three exit signs in the emergency
        product class. Each one carries the grade it earned and its roadmap to
        Full.
      </p>
      <div class="cf-table-wrap">
        <table class="cf-table">
          <colgroup>
            <col class="cf-col-record" />
            <col class="cf-col-grade" />
            <col class="cf-col-tostandard" />
            <col class="cf-col-tofull" />
          </colgroup>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Record</th>
              <th scope="col">Grade</th>
              <th scope="col">To Standard</th>
              <th scope="col">To Full</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {roadmap.map((r) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">{r.record}</th>
                <td data-label="Grade"><span class="cf-pill">{r.grade}</span></td>
                <td data-label="To Standard">{r.toStandard}</td>
                <td data-label="To Full">{r.toFull}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="cf-note">
        Vode is one field short of Standard. The two Lumenfacade records reach
        Standard with their DMX driver exempt from the analog-dimming rows, an
        applicability rule in action. The LPX exit sign is held at Core by two
        figures its spec sheet does not publish (letter height and self-powered
        input wattage), and the two edge-lit signs sit one tier below Full for the
        class-specific reason: Full asks for a test-report-backed face luminance,
        the UL 924 lab depth a spec sheet does not carry.
      </p>
    </section>

    {/* ── See your own roadmap ────────────────────────────────────────── */}
    <section class="cf-section">
      <h2 class="cf-h2">See your own roadmap</h2>
      <Callout intent="accent" label="Run the reference validator">
        <p>
          To see the roadmap for your own products, run the free reference
          validator: <code>ulc validate &lt;record&gt;</code>. Every record gets
          the grades it has already satisfied, a per-field list of exactly what it
          needs at each grade up to Full (each entry naming the source document and
          the governing standard), and a one-line achievements summary. Add
          <code>--verbose</code> for the non-gating enrichment roadmap and the
          per-theme achievements detail.
        </p>
        <p>
          The validator is a free download on the
          <a href="/downloads#cli" class="cf-link">downloads page</a>. To author a
          record in the first place, see
          <a href="/for-manufacturers#how-to-create" class="cf-link">how to create
          a ULC file</a>.
        </p>
      </Callout>
    </section>
  </SpecDocsLayout>
</PageShell>

<style>
  .page-h1 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 2.25rem;
    line-height: 1.1;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--text);
    margin: 0 0 1.25rem 0;
  }
  @media (min-width: 768px) { .page-h1 { font-size: 2.75rem; } }
  .page-intro {
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    margin: 0 0 1rem 0;
    max-width: 44rem;
  }

  .cf-section {
    margin-top: 3rem;
    scroll-margin-top: 5rem;
  }
  .cf-h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: 1.5rem;
    line-height: 1.2;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    color: var(--text);
    margin: 0 0 0.875rem 0;
  }
  @media (min-width: 768px) { .cf-h2 { font-size: 1.75rem; } }

  .cf-section p {
    font-size: 1rem;
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--text);
    margin: 1rem 0 0 0;
    max-width: 44rem;
  }
  .cf-section p:first-of-type {
    margin-top: 0;
  }
  .cf-section strong {
    color: var(--text);
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .cf-note {
    color: var(--text-muted) !important;
    font-size: 0.9375rem !important;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--border);
    padding-left: 1rem;
  }

  .cf-levels {
    margin: 1.25rem 0 0 0;
    max-width: 44rem;
  }
  .cf-levels dt {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--accent-warm);
    margin-top: 1.125rem;
  }
  .cf-levels dt:first-child {
    margin-top: 0;
  }
  .cf-levels dd {
    margin: 0.375rem 0 0 0;
    font-size: 1rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .cf-list {
    margin: 1.25rem 0 0 0;
    padding-left: 1.25rem;
    max-width: 44rem;
  }
  .cf-list li {
    font-size: 1rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    margin-top: 0.625rem;
  }
  .cf-list li:first-child {
    margin-top: 0;
  }
  .cf-list strong {
    color: var(--text);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .cf-table-wrap {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
    overflow-x: auto;
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 10px;
    /* Container context so the table reacts to its OWN width, not the viewport.
       The 3-pane spec layout leaves ~606px here on wide desktop AND a small
       phone leaves ~340px; a viewport query can't tell those apart, a
       container query can. */
    container-type: inline-size;
  }
  .cf-table {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    table-layout: fixed;
  }
  /* Deliberate column allocation for the ~606px main column the 3-pane spec
     layout leaves at >=1280px (sidebar 16rem + main + rail 14rem, capped at
     80rem). The record name and grade pill each get enough to sit on one line;
     the two roadmap columns take the rest. Padding is trimmed so four columns
     fit without the grade pill colliding with the next cell. overflow-wrap is
     a safety net only: columns are sized so real content never breaks mid-word. */
  .cf-col-record { width: 22%; }
  .cf-col-grade { width: 15%; }
  .cf-col-tostandard { width: 22%; }
  .cf-col-tofull { width: 41%; }
  .cf-table th,
  .cf-table td {
    text-align: left;
    vertical-align: top;
    padding: 0.75rem 0.625rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
    line-height: 1.5;
    overflow-wrap: break-word;
  }
  .cf-table thead th {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--text);
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    background: var(--bg-elevated);
  }
  .cf-table tbody th {
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--text);
  }
  .cf-table tbody td {
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }
  .cf-table tbody tr:last-child th,
  .cf-table tbody tr:last-child td {
    border-bottom: none;
  }
  /* Narrow container (phones): four columns never fit, so stack each record as a
     labelled card (matches the .alt-table / .proof-table pattern on
     /for-manufacturers). The data-label on each cell supplies the column name the
     hidden thead drops. Triggered by container width, so the ~606px desktop main
     column still gets the full table while a ~340px phone column gets cards. */
  @container (max-width: 560px) {
    .cf-table {
      table-layout: auto;
    }
    .cf-table thead {
      display: none;
    }
    .cf-table tbody tr {
      display: block;
      padding: 0.875rem 1rem;
      border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
    }
    .cf-table tbody tr:last-child {
      border-bottom: none;
    }
    .cf-table tbody th {
      display: block;
      padding: 0 0 0.5rem 0;
      border: none;
      font-size: 1rem;
    }
    .cf-table tbody td {
      display: block;
      padding: 0.375rem 0 0 0;
      border: none;
    }
    .cf-table tbody td::before {
      content: attr(data-label);
      display: block;
      font-size: 0.6875rem;
      font-variant-caps: all-small-caps;
      letter-spacing: 0.06em;
      color: var(--text-subtle);
      margin-bottom: 0.125rem;
    }
  }
  .cf-pill {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 0.125rem 0.5rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 999px;
    background: var(--bg);
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: 0.01em;
  }

  .cf-link {
    color: var(--accent-warm);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 3px;
  }
  .cf-link:hover {
    color: var(--accent-warm-deep);
  }
  .cf-section :global(code) {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.9em;
    color: var(--text);
  }
</style>
